title The dialect a guard assumes -- three Pond scans stop spelling GNU, and the loom that keeps them honest
prompt Open the lap as Hush, honor the ITINERARY Standing block, take the near door it names for this lane, and write the log on today's shelf.
think Opened with ListAgents and got no reachable peer, so the charter law from %291 -- name any peer before a file moves -- was satisfied by the empty answer rather than skipped. Then the roster cold, and held still while it ran.
think Rota row 4 from N = git rev-list --count HEAD = 3769. The previous lap read row 3, so no hand-advance. Earth, Mercury, the concrete -- the row whose sense is smell, the fact taken in whole at the door before any argument. That lens chose this lap's method before it chose its subject.
think REDS reads 0 OPEN rows, so standfast let constructive work begin. The card's near door for this lane names one concrete fact: xargs -r at pond_enclosure_door_scan.sh:158, a GNU extension BSD refuses.
think Rather than act on the report, I made it a measurement on this bench. A stand-in xargs that refuses -r the way BSD refuses it and falls through to the real tool otherwise, first on PATH, then the door control: no_home_named=no, control_verdict=failed. The claim became a reading without owning a Mac.
think Read what the flag was doing before replacing it -- guarding dirname against empty input. The shell's own [ -z ] carries the same guard and keeps the path whole besides, since xargs strips quotes and backslashes from what it reads, so a persist path holding either reached dirname already changed. The repair closes a latent mangling as well as the refusal.
think Same file, same class, six lines down: readlink -f at 241. Shimmed a readlink without -f and ran the scan on this bench, which has claude on PATH. It printed "no agent binary resolves on this bench" -- a machine fact, and a false one.
think Fixed the note rather than the value. Falling back to the unresolved path would have handed to_record a Homebrew path no record declares, and entry_unreachable is a ZERO-ENFORCED gate -- so the tidier-looking repair would have turned every macOS bench red. The reading stands down either way; it now stands down under the right name.
think Two instances of one class in one file is the loom threshold. The loom is a pen PATH whose xargs and readlink refuse every flag POSIX omits and fall through otherwise, with a stub claude so the entry branch is exercised the same way on a bench that has one and a bench that does not -- a dialect reading rather than a stub of the tool.
think Proved the loom from both sides. Against the elder scan on this GNU host it fails 2 readings; against the repair it passes. A guard proven only in the passing direction may be reading nothing at all.
think Widened by measurement rather than hunch. A static sweep of the whole Pond family for GNU-only spellings found one more: [ \t] inside a sed bracket expression in pond_enclosure_state_scan.sh read_pin. Tested it here -- GNU sed expands \t there, and outside GNU a backslash in a bracket is literal, so a BSD sed reads that class as space-or-backslash-or-t: it misses a real tab and eats a trailing t. [[:blank:]] matched GNU's behavior across five probes and is correct on both.
think Every repair was proven in pens outside the tree before a tracked byte moved, because the cold roster was still running and a run spread across two trees answers nothing.
obs The whole class is one sentence: a guard assumed the host's dialect instead of asking it. That is the capability tier's own lesson from one lap earlier, moved a layer down -- capability ipv6 asks what a host CAN DO, and this asks what it CAN SPELL. The answer is the same shape both times: probe, never assume.
obs Why it stayed invisible has a mechanism worth keeping. A portability gap in a FLAG is not a wrong number; it is a lost reading. Under set -e the scan dies before it can print anything, so the only bench that can see the fault is the bench that owns the dialect -- and that bench is the one least able to test a repair, because the tree's own tools are what refused. A shim on the bench that does NOT own the dialect is how the other bench reads for you. That is the transferable half.
obs The tree-wide grep is named here as the deliberate act read-scope asks for. `xargs -r` stood in exactly two living tracked files, and one of them was the card naming the other -- so the class was bounded before it was repaired rather than after.
obs The Earth row shaped the method without being asked to. Its threshold page says the concrete fact outranks the plausible story every time. The plausible story was that a reported Mac failure is enough to act on; the concrete fact was a control failing under a shim, which is a smaller claim and a far better one to hold.
obs What this lap did not reach, said plainly. Two of the three Mac Pond rows are dialect and are repaired here; the third needs a macOS reading, which is another body's bench. pond_policy_launcher_scan.sh carries [ \t] only inside awk regexes, where both awks read the escape as a tab, so it is not this class. And dialect is a different question from mechanism: bwrap is a Linux facility, and pond_enclosure_built_scan.sh already falls back to its pinned plan when ai-jail is absent, so a Mac row there would be an honest host tier rather than a repair.
obs Booked one REDS row for the class and closed nib_honesty without booking one. The nib the card carried, 53eaa7afa2, reads gone=1 -- the history rewrite that re-signed nine commits this evening took the commit the card pointed at, and the guard caught the dangling claim on the next lap. That is the ledger's ratchet class rather than a new defect, exactly as the previous lap named it.
obs My own red, and the more useful of the two. `caravan_suite` read red in the cold roster and green in the hot one on this pier, and a standalone quiet run reads GREEN in 12m03s across 121 witnesses. Observation: two rosters of one lap, 89/5 against 90/4, `caravan_suite` the single difference. Inference: the machine's load rather than the tree. I first wrote that as MY load alone -- three controls, two scans and five QA cards on four cores beside a 94-guard pass, against the Standing block's hold-still -- and then found a second `--hot` pass in the process table belonging to a peer body running its own 94-guard roster from its own clone at `/home/keeper/grain-dream`. So the claim is bounded rather than attributed: two loads were on the machine and nothing recorded either. Booked %348, after writing the false red into the card and having to withdraw it.
obs The finding under the finding, and it outlives this lap. `tree_moved=no` is a promise about ONE CHECKOUT. One tree per star (%281) removed the shared index and left the shared CPU, so six bodies contend for four cores where the roster's longest guard runs twelve minutes -- and a body inside its own clone cannot see that at all. The instrument this wants is small: a load reading recorded beside the digest, so a roster says what machine it read on and not only what tree.
obs I stopped my own hot roster rather than the peer's. Dream's started at 21:56 and mine at 22:06, and a lap that books a contention red and then wins the race it booked has learned nothing. The commit is safe from the %291 class regardless -- the peer holds its own clone, so no index is shared.
file tools/fixtures/p/pond_enclosure_door_scan.sh the two GNU-only flags become POSIX, and the entry note learns to tell an absent binary from an unfollowable one
file tools/fixtures/p/pond_enclosure_door_control.sh the loom -- a pen PATH whose xargs and readlink refuse every flag POSIX omits, two readings, proven from both sides
file tools/fixtures/p/pond_enclosure_state_scan.sh read_pin's sed bracket class becomes [[:blank:]], which names space and tab on every sed
file construction/REDS.md %347 books the dialect class with its three fields
file construction/ITINERARY.md the Now block records the repair, Next doors names what is left, and the Git nib stops pointing at a commit the evening's rewrite removed
loom control=pond_enclosure_door elder_gnu=ok elder_bsd_shim=failed failures=1 reading=no_home_named
loom control=pond_enclosure_door repaired_gnu=ok repaired_bsd_shim=ok new_readings=4
loom control=pond_enclosure_door new_readings_vs_elder_scan=failed failures=2 host=gnu
loom control=pond_enclosure_state elder_pass=24 elder_fail=0 repaired_pass=24 repaired_fail=0
loom sed_class_probes=5 gnu_bracket_t_vs_posix_blank=identical
loom roster=standing_equipment_run leg=cold guards=94 green=89 red=5 tree_moved=no wall_min=32 bench=loaded
loom roster=standing_equipment_run leg=hot guards=94 green=90 red=4 tree_moved=no wall_min=42 bench=quiet
loom roster_reds_hot=rule_twin,width_check,nib_honesty,remember_git_nib pond_guards=7 pond_green=7
loom witness=caravan_suite quiet_run=green wall_s=723 user_s=662 sys_s=320 rungs=121
loom bench=shared cores=4 concurrent_rosters=2 peer_clone=/home/keeper/grain-dream peer_started=21:56 mine_started=22:06 mine=stopped
recommend check-in (Claude) the near doors are named and the one worth a word is %348's remainder. Two instruments, both in the runner's own room, and the runner is what every body's lap opens on: a load reading recorded beside `tree_moved`, so a roster says what MACHINE it read on rather than only what tree; and a cleared red's evidence file kept under its stamp, since `construction/standing-equipment-reds/` holds the current run's reds alone and this lap could only infer what its cold `caravan_suite` red was. Both are small and neither is mine to seat alone, because six bodies read that runner. The mechanical doors beside them stay this seat's: `env`, the second of three door duties, and the third Mac Pond row, which wants a reading from a macOS body rather than another lap here.
